# Work started without waiting (`tick f(x)`), run until none of it can go further. A program with its own loop calls
# this between turns, the way a terminal app does after each key, so what a key started lands before the next paint.
#
# Every backend starts a ticked call at once and runs it to its first wait, as an async function called on node does.
# On node and in a browser the event loop finishes the rest between turns, so this is nothing there. On Rust what is
# still waiting is queued and this drains the queue (compile/rust.ts, `__term_drain`), on Swift this waits for the work
# still outstanding (compile/swift.ts, `__termDrain`), and on Kotlin it runs what the event loop holds (compile/kotlin.ts,
# `termLoop`). True always. test/compile/tick-native.ts holds all four.

mark unstable

bind run-pending
  like boolean
  case node
    text <true>
  case browser
    text <true>
  case rust
    text <\{ __term_drain(); true \}>
  case swift
    text <__termDrain()>
  case kotlin
    text <run \{ termLoop.drain(); true \}>
